### frankenscipy-9nw95 SUPERNODAL DENSE-PANEL BAR — RE-VERIFIED ON TODAY'S TREE (BlackThrush, 2026-08-23)

WHY THIS RUN EXISTS. I was asked to implement supernodal splu factorization with dense
panel updates, on the stated premise that supernodes/dense-panel kernels "have NO retry
predicate (never tried)". Both halves of that premise are false, and this file is the
check rather than the assertion:

  * They WERE tried. `frankenscipy-9nw95` is CLOSED with a measured mechanism, and the
    dense-scatter kernel is already in-tree — `dense_scatter_block_update`, staged behind
    `SPLU_SUPERNODAL_ENABLE`, deliberately not deleted.
  * There IS a retry predicate, stated in the closure: "BAR FOR ANY SUCCESSOR: must beat
    15.00 Ir per element-update, checkable with SUPERNODE_AB_WIDTH plus the committed
    workload harness."

So I ran that instrument instead of writing a fourth driver.

## COMMAND

  cargo test --release -p fsci-sparse --lib --no-run          (private target dir; the repo's
                                                               target/release is wedged, see
                                                               frankenscipy-y9bzw)
  SUPERNODE_AB_WIDTH=<w> valgrind --tool=callgrind \
    --callgrind-out-file=<out> <test-bin> dense_versus_merge --ignored --nocapture
  callgrind_annotate <out>

test binary sha256 = 41a0facfe4322d7ff708d47847f97170c41d1b128529822ff8b40fe6c06eec83
host=thinkstation1. Workload: span=300, repeats=2000, so element-updates = 2000*w*300.
Both kernels run over IDENTICAL data in ONE process, so callgrind's per-function
attribution is the comparison — no toggling, no separate binaries, no window.

## OBSERVED

  width   dense_scatter Ir   merge Ir        dense/merge   dense Ir/elem   merge Ir/elem
      2         71,574,054     53,730,102         1.3321          59.645          44.775
      5         98,656,054     80,730,102         1.2220          32.885          26.910
     12        161,908,054    143,730,102         1.1265          22.487          19.963
     24        270,340,054    251,730,102         1.0739          18.774          17.481

## WHAT IT SHOWS — slope and intercept separated

  width step   dense-minus-merge      dense slope/elem   merge slope/elem
      2->5           17,925,952                 15.046             15.000
     5->12           18,177,952                 15.060             15.000
    12->24           18,609,952                 15.060             15.000

The MARGINAL cost of the dense scatter is 15.06 Ir per element-update against the merge's
15.00 — reproducing the banked pair EXACTLY, on a binary built today. The gap between the
two kernels is a CONSTANT ~17.8-18.6M Ir, not a slope difference.

That is the whole verdict, and it is an ordering of two costs rather than a tuning failure:
identical slope plus a positive intercept means the ratio approaches 1.004 from ABOVE as
width grows and never crosses 1. THERE IS NO CROSSOVER WIDTH. Widening supernodes dilutes
the constant (1.3321x at w=2 down to 1.0739x at w=24) and can never pay it off.

## WHY "ADD SIMD TO THE DENSE PANEL" DOES NOT CLEAR THE BAR

The bar is on MARGINAL cost, which is where vectorisation would have to help. The
accumulate loop cannot vectorise, for two independent reasons that are structural rather
than effort-limited:

  1. It is an indexed SCATTER: `accumulator[col as usize]` with `col` read from
     `tail_cols`. Without AVX-512 gather/scatter there is no vector form, and this repo
     deliberately excludes avx512 from `.cargo/config.toml` so binaries stay portable
     across the whole rch fleet (no sampled worker reports AVX-512).
  2. Every element carries a data-dependent early return —
     `if updated == 0.0 && previous != 0.0 { return false }` — which the BIT-IDENTITY
     contract requires, because an existing entry driven to zero while pivots remain must
     be replayed sequentially. A loop-carried exit blocks vectorisation outright, and
     removing it would break the contract the whole line of work is built on.

Making that loop vectorisable needs the factor stored in dense blocks so the tail is
contiguous — the rewrite the bead already priced, which "invalidates the SortedFactorRow
representation several landed levers are built on". And the reason it still would not pay
is recorded in the closure: the merge kernel is ALREADY dense inside its runs (96.8% of
elements), so blocking re-densifies something that was never sparse.

## VERDICT

The retry predicate is NOT met. The successor bar (beat 15.00 Ir/element-update) is missed
by the existing dense kernel at 15.06, and the one proposed mechanism for closing it
(SIMD) is blocked by the storage representation and by the bit-identity contract. No
fourth supernodal driver was written.

NOT ESTABLISHED HERE: anything about wall-clock, about the incumbent, or about whether a
full dense-block STORAGE rewrite (as opposed to the staged scatter kernel) would change
the slope. That rewrite remains priced but unbuilt, and its own pre-costing already says
the structure it would exploit is not the thing that is slow.
